{"id":"sat-percentages-ratios-data","title":"SAT Math: percentages, ratios and data","subject":"Mathematics","label":"SAT","exams":["sat"],"language":"en","seconds":187,"duration":186.66,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":497,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"The puzzle","start":0,"end":14.545},{"id":"explain","title":"Percents","start":14.545,"end":59.115},{"id":"explain2","title":"Ratios and data","start":59.115,"end":87.145},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":87.145,"end":113.855},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":113.855,"end":123.035},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":123.035,"end":131.645},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":131.645,"end":154.305},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":154.305,"end":186.66}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":6.95,"text":"A jacket is marked twenty five percent off, then tax is added. Does the discount apply before tax?","w":[0.58,0.7,1.16,1.3,1.64,1.96,2.22,2.66,3.38,3.6,3.94,4.12,4.96,5.18,5.3,5.78,6.14,6.46]},{"s":0,"a":7.4,"b":14.12,"text":"And why don't two ten percent changes cancel out? I'm Kora, and today we make these SAT questions simple.","w":[7.46,7.64,7.88,8.16,8.38,8.6,8.92,9.3,9.72,10.7,10.88,11.54,11.66,12,12.16,12.34,12.76,13.22,13.76]},{"s":1,"a":14.97,"b":21.52,"text":"Percent means per hundred. So thirty percent is thirty out of one hundred, or zero point three zero as a decimal.","w":[15,15.5,15.78,15.94,16.8,17.18,17.46,17.98,18.16,18.48,18.6,18.72,18.86,19.58,19.74,20.06,20.32,20.58,21,21.1,21.16]},{"s":1,"a":21.82,"b":30.61,"text":"To find a percent of a number, multiply by the decimal. Thirty percent of eighty is zero point three zero times eighty, which equals twenty four.","w":[21.84,21.98,22.28,22.34,22.82,22.94,23.04,23.92,24.54,24.68,24.8,25.7,26,26.44,26.64,27.04,27.14,27.48,27.82,28.16,28.52,28.92,29.42,29.66,30,30.3]},{"s":1,"a":30.91,"b":37.91,"text":"Percent change is new minus old, divided by old, times one hundred. Always divide by the original value.","w":[30.96,31.4,31.76,31.94,32.26,32.68,33.26,33.7,34,34.52,34.98,35.14,36.06,36.4,36.8,36.94,37.08,37.48]},{"s":1,"a":38.21,"b":46.26,"text":"For shortcuts, a twenty percent increase multiplies by one point two zero. A twenty percent decrease multiplies by zero point eight zero.","w":[38.24,38.4,39.3,39.38,39.68,40.12,40.58,41.26,41.52,41.72,42.02,42.22,43.04,43.14,43.42,43.8,44.3,44.94,45.12,45.46,45.74,45.92]},{"s":1,"a":46.56,"b":54.08,"text":"Two changes in a row multiply. Up ten percent then down ten percent is one point one zero times zero point nine zero.","w":[46.6,46.88,47.3,47.4,47.5,47.78,48.98,49.14,49.36,50.3,50.54,50.84,51.04,51.5,51.7,51.86,52.18,52.34,52.66,52.96,53.28,53.54,53.78]},{"s":1,"a":54.38,"b":58.69,"text":"That gives zero point nine nine, a one percent decrease, not zero.","w":[54.4,54.6,54.84,55.26,55.6,55.9,56.36,56.54,56.74,57.14,58.06,58.3]},{"s":2,"a":59.54,"b":68.13,"text":"Now ratios. Write two equal fractions with the same units, then cross multiply. A unit rate compares to one, like miles per hour.","w":[59.6,59.86,60.62,60.92,61.24,61.54,62.06,62.22,62.34,62.66,63.26,63.54,63.88,65.16,65.34,65.6,65.86,66.34,66.6,66.92,67.24,67.64,67.78]},{"s":2,"a":68.43,"b":73.36,"text":"If a car travels one hundred fifty miles in three hours, that is fifty miles per hour.","w":[68.48,68.6,68.7,69,69.52,69.68,70,70.32,70.72,70.84,71.1,71.86,72.06,72.24,72.58,72.92,73.08]},{"s":2,"a":73.66,"b":78.9,"text":"For data, mean is sum divided by count. Median is the middle value after you order the numbers.","w":[73.7,73.88,74.24,74.5,74.64,74.92,75.34,75.52,76.32,76.72,76.84,76.98,77.26,77.72,77.94,78.12,78.36,78.46]},{"s":2,"a":79.2,"b":86.72,"text":"An outlier pulls the mean much more than the median. Before any graph or table question, read the axis labels and units.","w":[79.24,79.48,80.02,80.38,80.54,80.94,81.24,81.56,81.7,81.82,82.74,83.14,83.36,83.82,83.94,84.26,85.02,85.26,85.48,85.84,86.24,86.42]},{"s":3,"a":87.57,"b":95.16,"text":"Here is a worked example. A jacket costs eighty dollars. It is twenty five percent off, then eight percent sales tax is added.","w":[87.6,87.86,87.98,88.06,88.38,89.4,89.52,89.94,90.44,90.7,91.52,91.62,91.76,92.06,92.3,92.72,93.22,93.5,93.7,94.14,94.44,94.72,94.9]},{"s":3,"a":95.46,"b":103.03,"text":"First, twenty five percent off means you pay seventy five percent. Eighty times zero point seven five equals sixty dollars.","w":[95.48,96.22,96.52,96.76,97.16,97.36,97.6,97.74,98.02,98.38,98.62,99.5,99.8,100.18,100.54,100.8,101.1,101.84,102.2,102.54]},{"s":3,"a":103.33,"b":109.88,"text":"Then add eight percent tax. Sixty times one point zero eight equals sixty four dollars and eighty cents.","w":[103.4,103.8,104.04,104.26,104.64,105.48,105.84,106.24,106.44,106.72,107.08,107.62,107.96,108.3,108.52,108.98,109.16,109.44]},{"s":3,"a":110.18,"b":113.43,"text":"Notice we multiplied the two changes instead of adding them.","w":[110.28,110.66,110.88,111.48,111.62,111.88,112.4,112.76,112.9,113.14]},{"s":4,"a":114.28,"b":120.05,"text":"Quick check. A price goes from fifty dollars to sixty dollars. What is the percent increase?","w":[114.32,114.58,115.32,115.44,115.8,116.08,116.32,116.64,117.04,117.22,117.58,118.56,118.76,118.9,119.02,119.48]},{"s":4,"a":120.5,"b":122.61,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[120.54,120.88,121.18,121.32,121.62,122.14,122.3]},{"s":5,"a":123.46,"b":131.22,"text":"The answer is twenty percent. The change is ten dollars, and ten divided by the original fifty is zero point two zero, or twenty percent.","w":[123.48,123.66,124.04,124.2,124.5,125.32,125.48,125.96,126.12,126.4,127.22,127.38,127.64,128.04,128.16,128.3,128.7,129.14,129.28,129.6,129.86,130.06,130.46,130.6,130.88]},{"s":6,"a":132.07,"b":139.7,"text":"Common mistakes. First, percent change divides by the original value, not the new one. Underline the starting number before you calculate.","w":[132.12,132.5,133.42,133.84,134.24,134.62,135.1,135.26,135.38,135.8,136.24,136.44,136.56,136.78,137.44,137.94,138.08,138.46,138.74,139.04,139.18]},{"s":6,"a":140,"b":148.29,"text":"Second, do not add percents that should be multiplied. Twenty five percent off, then eight percent tax, is not seventeen percent off.","w":[140.1,140.88,141.04,141.36,141.56,142,142.16,142.36,142.5,143.56,143.88,144.14,144.58,145.18,145.48,145.7,146.08,146.82,146.94,147.2,147.7,148.08]},{"s":6,"a":148.59,"b":153.88,"text":"Third, keep mean and median separate, and always check the units on a graph or table.","w":[148.64,149.38,149.68,150.06,150.18,150.62,151.46,151.7,152.02,152.28,152.44,152.76,152.86,152.94,153.38,153.52]},{"s":7,"a":154.73,"b":164.57,"text":"Recap. Percent means per hundred, and percent change divides by the original value. Multipliers handle increases and decreases, and two changes multiply.","w":[154.76,155.48,155.88,156.22,156.42,157.3,157.46,157.88,158.24,158.68,158.82,158.94,159.36,160.3,161,161.38,162,162.14,163.18,163.38,163.64,164.08]},{"s":7,"a":164.87,"b":173.12,"text":"Ratios use equal fractions with matching units. For data, mean is sum divided by count, and median is the middle ordered value.","w":[164.92,165.58,165.84,166.18,166.7,166.88,167.32,168.14,168.34,169,169.4,169.54,169.82,170.28,170.48,171.24,171.38,171.86,172,172.12,172.44,172.7]},{"s":7,"a":173.42,"b":175.88,"text":"Outliers pull the mean more than the median.","w":[173.54,174.2,174.46,174.6,174.98,175.18,175.34,175.44]},{"s":7,"a":176.18,"b":181.95,"text":"Exam tip: read labels and units before you calculate, and check whether you need the original value.","w":[176.24,176.6,177.32,177.56,178,178.2,178.54,178.88,179.04,180.04,180.18,180.44,180.7,180.84,181.02,181.14,181.54]},{"s":7,"a":182.25,"b":186.06,"text":"Keep practicing with Knowzone, and these questions will feel much easier.","w":[182.28,182.52,183.04,183.18,184.16,184.28,184.5,184.9,185.1,185.42,185.66]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"The puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"25% off, then tax: discount before tax?"},{"at":7.3,"kind":"text","text":"Why don't two 10% changes cancel?"}]},{"at":14.72,"heading":"Percents","items":[{"at":14.87,"kind":"term","text":"Percent","detail":"per hundred; 30% = 30/100 = 0.30"},{"at":21.72,"kind":"formula","text":"30% of 80 = 0.30 × 80 = 24"},{"at":30.81,"kind":"formula","text":"Change% = (new − old) ÷ old × 100"}]},{"at":37.96,"heading":"Multipliers","items":[{"at":38.11,"kind":"formula","text":"20% increase → ×1.20"},{"at":38.11,"kind":"formula","text":"20% decrease → ×0.80"},{"at":46.46,"kind":"formula","text":"1.10 × 0.90"},{"at":54.28,"kind":"formula","text":"= 0.99 → 1% decrease"}]},{"at":59.29,"heading":"Ratios & data","items":[{"at":59.44,"kind":"term","text":"Ratio setup","detail":"equal fractions, same units; cross multiply"},{"at":59.44,"kind":"term","text":"Unit rate","detail":"compares to one, like miles per hour"},{"at":68.33,"kind":"formula","text":"150 mi ÷ 3 h = 50 mi/h"},{"at":73.56,"kind":"formula","text":"Mean = sum/count; median = middle value"},{"at":79.1,"kind":"text","text":"Outliers pull mean more than median; read axes/units"}]},{"at":87.32,"heading":"Worked example","items":[{"at":87.47,"kind":"text","text":"Jacket $80; 25% off, then 8% tax"},{"at":95.36,"kind":"step","text":"80 × 0.75 = 60"},{"at":103.23,"kind":"step","text":"60 × 1.08 = 64.80"},{"at":110.08,"kind":"text","text":"Multiply changes; don't add percents"}]},{"at":113.86,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":113.86,"kind":"question","text":"A price goes from $50 to $60. What is the percent increase?"}]},{"at":123.21,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":123.36,"kind":"formula","text":"Change = 60 − 50 = 10"},{"at":123.36,"kind":"formula","text":"10 ÷ 50 = 0.20 = 20%"}]},{"at":131.82,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":131.97,"kind":"wrong","text":"Divide by new value"},{"at":131.97,"kind":"right","text":"Divide by the original value"},{"at":139.9,"kind":"wrong","text":"25% off + 8% tax = 17% off"},{"at":139.9,"kind":"right","text":"Multiply: 0.75 × 1.08"},{"at":148.49,"kind":"wrong","text":"Mix mean and median; ignore units"}]},{"at":154.48,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":154.63,"kind":"term","text":"Percent change","detail":"Divide by original; multipliers multiply"},{"at":164.77,"kind":"text","text":"Ratios: equal fractions, matching units"},{"at":173.32,"kind":"text","text":"Outliers pull mean more than median"},{"at":176.08,"kind":"tip","text":"Read labels and units; check if you need the original value"},{"at":182.15,"kind":"text","text":"Keep practicing with Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":122.86,"seconds":10,"question":"A price goes from $50 to $60. What is the percent increase?","answer":"20%: change = $10; 10 ÷ 50 = 0.20 = 20%"},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}